    "failure_scenario": "Any future edit to the verification protocol (e.g. tightening the 'name the EXACT broken invariant' wording or the hard-cap phrasing) must be hand-applied to both strings in lockstep; miss one and the Opus and generic compact arms silently diverge in policy. The test suite even encodes this fragility — test_opus_cli_compact_pins_token_discipline exists specifically because the two branches can drift. A shared f-string template with the per-variant limit/cap interpolated would collapse both branches and remove the drift surface entirely."
  },
  {
    "file": "benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py",
    "line": 1735,
    "summary": "no_brain is guarded twice: at the call site (line 3315: `if condition != \"no_brain\" and ...`) AND inside capture_brief_packet (line 1735: `if str(condition) == \"no_brain\": return`). The inner guard is also redundant with the CLI_BRIEF_CONDITIONS gate three lines later, since no_brain is not in that set and would return anyway.",
    "failure_scenario": "Three overlapping guards for one invariant (no_brain never captures): a reader must reconcile call-site + early-return + set-membership to confirm there's no contradiction, and a maintainer changing the policy in one place may leave the others stale. The 'defense-in-depth' comment justifies keeping the function self-contained, but the no_brain early-return is strictly subsumed by `str(condition) in CLI_BRIEF_CONDITIONS` (no_brain ∉ set), so it is dead given the very next check — pure redundant code to maintain."
  },
  {
    "file": "benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py",
    "line": 1779,
    "summary": "Inline stdout-bounding (stdout_cap = 200_000; head-slice + len-based stdout_truncated flag) is hand-rolled here, while the codebase uniformly bounds subprocess output with tail-slices elsewhere (proc.stderr[-4000:], proc.stdout[-4000:] at lines 1670-1671, plus stderr[-2000:]/[-3000:] across collect_brain_state, run_one, etc.). There is no shared truncation helper and this is the only head-slice + explicit-truncation-flag instance.",
    "failure_scenario": "The output-bounding policy is now scattered across ~8 call sites with magic constants (4000/3000/2000/200_000) and inconsistent direction (head here, tail everywhere else). A reviewer cannot tell from one site what the project's truncation convention is, and a future change to caps/flagging must be chased across all sites. A single `bound_output(text, cap, keep='head'|'tail') -> (text, truncated)` helper would let capture_brief_packet, collect_brain_state, and the prep loop share one policy instead of each re-deriving it."
  },
  {
    "file": "benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py",
    "line": 1714,
    "summary": "capture_brief_packet's docstring is ~25 lines (lines 1714-1734) and the function body adds another ~6 lines of block comments (1738-1742, 1778-1779) re-stating the same CLI_BRIEF_CONDITIONS / shlex-mirror / cgo-cache-warming rationale that already appears verbatim in the CLI_BRIEF_CONDITIONS definition comment (lines 264-270) and the prompt_for shlex comment (lines 2350-2355).",
    "failure_scenario": "The same three rationales (closed-set gate prevents over-exposure, shlex.quote makes both channels identical, extra subprocess can warm the embedding cache) are now stated 3-4 times across the file. For an off-by-default diagnostic helper, this is a large comment-to-code ratio: any change to the gating or quoting logic requires editing four prose copies to keep them consistent, and the volume buries the ~30 lines of actual logic. One canonical rationale near CLI_BRIEF_CONDITIONS with one-line pointers from the call sites would carry the same information at a fraction of the maintenance cost."
  },
  {
    "file": "benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py",
    "line": 2360,
    "summary": "opus_brief_command is unconditionally built on every prompt_for call (line 2360) but, per its own comment, is consumed only inside the single `full_cli_compact and is_opus and semantic_available` branch (line 2417). Likewise _generic_limit/brief_limit/brief_command are computed for every condition even though no_brain, all mcp_* branches, and the disabled-semantic branches never interpolate them.",
    "failure_scenario": "Wasted string-formatting work (an extra brain_brief_limit call + f-string with embedded shlex.quote) on every prompt build for the majority of conditions that never use opus_brief_command. Minor in cost, but it also misleads: a reader sees opus_brief_command and brief_command computed at top level and must scan the whole if/elif chain to discover opus_brief_command has exactly one consumer. Building these lazily inside (or just before) the two branches that use them would localize the only place each command matters."
  },
  {
    "file": "benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py",
    "line": 1773,
    "summary": "capture_brief_packet re-builds the brief argv inline (`args = [str(tools[\"brain\"]), \"brief\", brief_query, \"--json\"]` + conditional `--limit`) which is the exact argv-equivalent of the shell string prompt_for assembles (`entire brain brief {query} --json{limit}`). The two command constructions are kept in sync only by the shared brain_brief_query/brain_brief_limit helpers plus a lockstep test, not by a single command-builder.",
    "failure_scenario": "The brief command shape (subcommand name 'brief', '--json' flag, '--limit' flag spelling/order) is duplicated across prompt_for (shell form) and capture_brief_packet (argv form). A future flag change (e.g. renaming --json or adding a default flag) must be applied to both forms and could pass brain_brief_query/limit checks while still diverging on the surrounding flags. A shared `brief_argv(tools, query, limit) -> list[str]` (with prompt_for shlex.join-ing it) would make the mirror structural rather than test-enforced — the test_capture_brief_packet_mirrors_prompt_for invariant exists precisely because this is currently only convention."
  }
]

